    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The buzz cut is the simplest haircut there is and still easy to get wrong. Go one guard too short and your scalp shows through under bright light; go one too long and it looks like a grown-out haircut rather than a deliberate one. The fix is knowing what each guard number actually leaves on your head, choosing a style that suits the shape of your skull and your hairline, and keeping a regular trim schedule. Get those three right and a pair of clippers replaces the barber for most of the year.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="guard-chart" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Clipper Guard Sizes, in Plain Numbers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clipper guards are numbered in eighths of an inch on most American clippers. A #1 leaves about an eighth of an inch, a #2 a quarter inch, a #4 half an inch and a #8 a full inch. In metric, each step is roughly 3 mm, with a few irregular jumps. Wahl, Andis and Oster follow the same basic system, though exact lengths can vary by a millimeter between brands, so stick to one brand's guards for consistent results.
          </p>

          <p>
            Below #1 are the half sizes. A #0.5 leaves about 1.5 mm, and a #1.5 about 4.5 mm, which is useful for blending. With no guard at all, most clippers cut around 0.5 to 1 mm with the taper lever closed and closer to 2 to 3 mm with it open. Shavers and foil tools go closer still, to essentially bare skin.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you read hair length in millimeters, remember that hair lies down as it grows, so a 6 mm cut does not look like 6 mm of standing hair. Coarse, straight hair stands up and looks longer; fine or curly hair lies flatter and shows more scalp. Use the chart as a starting point and adjust by one guard after you see the result.
          </p>

