        <div id="pick-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Picking the Right Length
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start one or two guards longer than you think you want. You can always go shorter in a minute, but going back up takes two to three weeks of regrowth. A first buzz cut at #3 or #4 shows you how your head shape and any scalp scars or birthmarks look before you commit to a #1 or #2.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hair color</strong> changes how short you can go. Dark hair on light skin gives a strong contrast, so even a #1 reads as a solid, deliberate cut. Blond, gray or very light hair shows more scalp at the same length and can look patchy below a #2. Darker skin with dark hair shows less contrast, so very short cuts look clean and even.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hair density and texture</strong> matter too. Thick, coarse hair stands up and looks fuller at any length, so it suits short buzzes well. Fine or thin hair looks best either very short, where the difference between hair and scalp is minimal, or at #3 to #4, where the length hides density gaps. The in-between lengths are where thinning shows most.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Work and climate</strong> are practical tie-breakers. Very short cuts are cooler in summer and under helmets, and they dry instantly after a swim or workout. They also expose your scalp to sun and cold. If you spend hours outdoors, plan on a hat or scalp sunscreen at #2 and shorter.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SAFE START</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">First buzz cut? Use a #3 and step down from there.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A #3 looks deliberate on almost everyone, hides most scalp scars and grows out without an awkward stage. If you like it, go to a #2 next time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="styles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Main Buzz Cut Styles
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Induction cut</strong>: no guard at all, the whole head at the clipper's closest setting. It is the military recruit look, very clean and bold, and it shows every bump and scar. <strong>Burr cut</strong>: a uniform #1 or #2, just enough hair to give the head color. It is the most common buzz cut, and the one most people mean when they say buzz.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Butch cut</strong>: a uniform #3 or #4, long enough to show texture and soften the head's outline. It works well for men who want a buzz without the shaved look. <strong>Crew cut</strong>: longer on top, often #4 to #6, and shorter on the sides and back, usually tapered. It is technically a buzz-adjacent style, more forgiving for head shape and easy to do with two guard sizes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>High and tight</strong>: very short or skin on the sides and back, up to the top of the head, with a short #1 to #3 strip on top. It is a strong military-style look. <strong>Buzz cut with fade</strong>: any uniform top length with the sides graduated shorter toward the neck and ears, which sharpens the shape and makes the cut look barber-finished.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Line-up or edge-up</strong> finishes any of these styles by squaring off the hairline at the forehead, temples and neck with a trimmer. It turns a basic home buzz into something that looks professionally kept. Use caution at the front hairline, though: pushing the line back to straighten it can make a receding hairline more obvious later.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
