                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Graduated</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Every 1 to 2 weeks</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="head-shape" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Head Shape, Face Shape and Hairline
          </h2>

          <p>
            A buzz cut removes the hair that normally rounds off the head, so head shape matters more than face shape. Run your hand over the back and crown and look at your profile in two mirrors. A flat back of the head, a pronounced ridge or an uneven crown shows more at #1 than at #3. A slightly longer top with shorter sides, a crew cut, helps balance a flat back or a narrow crown.
          </p>

          <p>
            Face shape is secondary but still useful. Strong jaws and square or oval faces suit very short buzz cuts because the hair does not compete with the bone structure. Round faces often look better with a fade that keeps the sides tight, adding height and angles through contrast. Long faces benefit from keeping the sides a little fuller rather than skin-faded, which keeps the head from looking taller.
          </p>

          <p>
            Facial hair changes the picture. A short beard or heavy stubble adds weight to the lower face and balances a very short buzz, especially on rounder faces. Blend the sideburns into the beard with a guard one size longer than the head, so there is no hard line at the ear. If you go clean-shaven, a slightly longer buzz softens the overall look.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at your hairline in a bright overhead light before choosing a length. A widow's peak or deep temple recession is very visible at #1 and nearly invisible at #3. If you plan a line-up, follow your natural hairline rather than squaring it off, and never take the front line back further than it already sits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="thinning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Buzz Cuts and Thinning Hair
          </h2>

          <p>
            A short buzz is one of the best cuts for thinning hair. When hair is long, thinning areas show as gaps between strands and a contrast between full sides and a sparse crown. When everything is cut short, the difference in density between areas shrinks, and the eye reads the head as one even shape.
          </p>

          <p>
            Go shorter as thinning progresses. Men with mild thinning at the crown often look best at #2 or #3. With visible thinning across the top, a #1 or #0.5 makes the difference between thick and thin areas minimal. When the top is mostly bare, many men shave the whole head instead, which is cleaner than leaving a short fringe around the sides.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the sides no longer than the top. A common mistake is leaving fuller sides to compensate, which actually draws attention to the thin top. Match lengths or go shorter on the sides with a fade, so the top looks relatively fuller.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are using hair loss treatments such as minoxidil, short hair makes application easier and lets the product reach the scalp. Discuss any treatment with a doctor or dermatologist, particularly prescription options. A short cut also makes it easier to see whether a treatment is helping over months, since you are comparing like with like.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Clippers and the Rest of the Kit
          </h2>

          <p>
            A decent set of clippers pays for itself within a few cuts. Look for a powerful motor, a full set of guards from #0.5 to #8, and a taper lever for half-step adjustments. Corded clippers with magnetic or pivot motors, such as long-running models from Wahl and Andis, are reliable and never die mid-cut. Cordless clippers make reaching the back of your head easier, so choose one with a lithium-ion battery that runs at least an hour per charge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Guard quality matters more than people expect. Thin plastic guards flex as you push through thick hair, cutting shorter in some passes than others. Sturdier guards, or guards with metal clips, hold the length consistently. Keep the guards from the same brand as the clipper, because universal guards can sit unevenly on the blade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add a T-blade trimmer for the hairline, neck and around the ears; it is narrower and cuts closer than a clipper. A hand mirror or a three-way mirror, or a phone propped up to record the back, solves the visibility problem. Clipper oil, a cleaning brush and blade disinfectant spray keep the tools sharp and clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan your cleanup before you start. Clipped hair gets everywhere, so cut in the bathroom or on a smooth floor rather than carpet. Tile and <a href="/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/">vinyl plank</a> sweep clean in a minute, and a small wet-dry <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac</a> picks up the fine bits that brooms push around. A cape or an old towel around the neck keeps hair off your clothes.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Kit Costs</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$30-$90</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Home clipper kit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$30-$70</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">T-blade trimmer</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$25-$45</p>
