              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-4 cuts</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">To break even on a kit</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="diy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cutting a Buzz Cut Yourself
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with clean, completely dry hair. Wet hair clumps and cuts unevenly, and some clippers are not designed to be used on wet hair. Comb it out so it stands away from the scalp. Oil the blade with a few drops, run it for a few seconds, and wipe off the excess. Attach your chosen guard and check that it clicks firmly in place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut against the direction of growth. On most heads, that means moving from the nape up toward the crown at the back, from the bottom of the sides up toward the top, and from the forehead back over the top. Use slow, overlapping passes with the guard flat against the scalp. Cover every area twice, from different directions, because hair grows in swirls around the crown.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work in a fixed order so nothing gets missed: top, then right side, then left side, then the back. Use a mirror or record the back with your phone to check for uneven patches. Run your hand over the head; longer hairs stand out by feel even when you cannot see them. Go over those areas again with the same guard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish the edges. Take the guard off, or use the trimmer, to tidy around the ears and the neckline. For the neck, decide on a rounded, squared or tapered back line; tapered grows out most naturally. Hold a hand mirror to see the back while you trim, and stay at or below the natural hairline so you do not create a step in the cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse or brush off loose hair, check once more in good light, and clean the clipper. Brush hair out of the blade and guard, spray with blade disinfectant, and oil the blade before storing it. A clean, oiled blade cuts more smoothly and lasts much longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Adding a Fade or Taper at Home
          </h2>

          <p>
            A fade takes a buzz cut from basic to sharp, and a simple one is doable at home with patience. Choose your top length, say a #3, and cut the entire head with it first. Then decide where the fade starts: a low fade begins just above the ears, a mid fade about halfway up the sides, and a high fade near the temple line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work down in steps. With one guard size shorter than the top, a #2, cut the sides and back up to a little below where you want the top length to start, flicking the clipper outward at the top of each pass. Then go one size shorter, a #1, and cut a lower band. Then a #0.5 or the open taper lever for the lowest band near the ears and nape.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blend the lines. Each step leaves a visible line where the guard sizes meet. Use the half sizes, or the taper lever, to soften those lines, working with a scooping motion that pulls the clipper away from the head as you go up. Small, gentle passes are better than big ones; you can always remove more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the fade even on both sides by using landmarks such as the top of the ear, the temple corner and the occipital bone at the back. Check symmetry in a mirror from the front. If one side ends up higher, raise the other to match rather than trying to grow it back. Skin fades with a foil shaver at the bottom look clean but need weekly upkeep.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Upkeep and Scalp Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hair grows about half an inch a month, roughly 1 to 1.5 cm, so a buzz cut changes visibly within a week or two. A #1 to #2 looks best touched up every 7 to 10 days, a #3 to #4 every two to three weeks. Fades and line-ups lose their sharpness fastest and benefit from a quick trimmer pass between full cuts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your scalp becomes your hairstyle. Wash it regularly with a gentle shampoo, since short hair does not hide oil or flakes. If you have dandruff, a medicated shampoo with ingredients like zinc pyrithione or ketoconazole helps. Moisturize the scalp if it gets tight or dry in winter, and choose a non-greasy, fragrance-free lotion.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect it from the sun. With very short hair, the scalp burns easily, and sun damage on the scalp is common. Use a broad-spectrum sunscreen of SPF 30 or higher, in spray or lightweight lotion form, or wear a hat for long periods outdoors. In cold weather, a hat keeps the scalp from chapping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the rest of your grooming in step. A neat buzz with an overgrown neck, shaggy sideburns or a hairy back peeking above a collar undoes the effect. Trim the neck and sideburns with every cut, and if body hair is part of the picture, a long-handled tool from our <a href="/best-mens-back-shavers/">back shaver picks</a> takes care of the parts you cannot reach.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grow-out" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Growing a Buzz Cut Out
          </h2>

          <p>
            Growing out a buzz cut takes patience more than skill. From a #1 or #2, expect about two months to reach a short, styleable crop and four to six months for a short classic cut with some length on top. Longer styles, such as hair you can push back or part, usually take nine months to a year or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            The awkward stage is usually around six to ten weeks, when hair is too long to look like a buzz and too short to style. The fix is to keep the sides and back trimmed while the top grows. A barber can take the sides down to a #2 or #3 every few weeks, so the shape stays neat and only the top gains length.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hair rarely grows in evenly. Cowlicks at the crown and front, which were invisible at a #1, reappear as hair lengthens. A small amount of matte paste or clay helps hold unruly areas once you have an inch or so on top. Keep the neckline tidy throughout, which alone makes growing hair look intentional.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you decide you prefer it short after all, nothing is lost. Most men who try a buzz cut for a summer either keep it or come back to it. Keep the clipper kit, the guards and the trimmer clean and stored, and you can return to a buzz in ten minutes whenever you like.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting with the shortest guard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Going straight to a #1 on a first buzz cut leaves no room to adjust if your head shape or scalp surprises you. Start at #3, look at it for a few days, then step down. Shorter is a minute away; longer is weeks away.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting wet or dirty hair</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wet hair clumps and lies flat, so the clipper misses strands, and greasy hair clogs the guard. Wash and fully dry your hair first, comb it upward, and the cut comes out even in a single session instead of patchy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressing the guard into the scalp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pushing hard tilts the guard and makes some areas shorter than others, especially at the crown. Hold the clipper flat with light pressure and move slowly so the guard can lift and feed the hair. Go over every area twice from different directions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaving the hairline back</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Squaring the front hairline with a trimmer looks crisp for a week, but pushing it back makes a receding hairline look worse and it takes weeks to regrow. Follow your natural hairline and only clean up stray hairs below it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping sunscreen on the scalp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A short buzz exposes skin that has spent years under hair. It burns quickly and is a common site for sun damage. Use an SPF 30 or higher spray or light lotion on sunny days, or wear a hat for long stretches outdoors.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which guard number is a standard buzz cut?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A #2, which leaves about a quarter inch or 6 mm, is the most common buzz cut length. A #1 at 3 mm is a shorter, sharper version, and a #3 at about 10 mm is a softer buzz that shows less scalp. Different brands vary slightly, so stick with one clipper brand's guards for consistent results.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long until a buzz cut grows back?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hair grows about half an inch a month. From a #1 or #2, expect around two months to a short crop you can style a little, and four to six months for a classic short cut. Longer styles take nine months to a year. Keep the sides trimmed while the top grows to avoid an awkward stage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it hard to buzz your own head?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not with the right kit and a method. Use clean, dry hair, a sharp oiled clipper and a firmly attached guard, cut against the grain in slow overlapping passes, and check the back with a mirror or phone camera. Uniform buzzes are easy; fades take some practice but are realistic after a few attempts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a buzz cut make thinning hair look worse?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually the opposite. Cutting everything short reduces the contrast between thicker and thinner areas, so the head looks more even. Very short lengths like a #1 or #0.5 hide thinning best. Keep the sides at the same length or shorter than the top, because longer sides draw attention to a thin crown.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if a buzz cut will suit me?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check your head shape in two mirrors and feel for flat spots, ridges or scars. Start with a #3, which flatters most head shapes and hides minor bumps, and go shorter later if you like it. Strong jaws and oval or square faces suit very short cuts; round faces often benefit from a fade on the sides.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a buzz cut be trimmed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a #1 or #2, every 7 to 10 days keeps it crisp. A #3 or #4 can go two to three weeks. Fades and line-ups lose definition fastest and benefit from a quick touch-up with a trimmer between full cuts. Trim the neckline and sideburns every time, since those areas look untidy first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a buzz cut and a crew cut?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A buzz cut is usually one uniform length all over, cut with a single guard. A crew cut keeps the top longer, often #4 to #6, and takes the sides and back shorter with a taper or fade. A crew cut is more forgiving for head shape and slightly more styled, while a buzz is simpler to maintain.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

